# 3️⃣ Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA)

## 📖 Definition

The Lowest Common Ancestor of two nodes is the lowest node in the tree that has both nodes as descendants.

Example:

```text
          1
        /   \
       2     3
      / \
     4   5
```

For:

```text
4 and 5
```

LCA:

```text
2
```

For:

```text
4 and 3
```

LCA:

```text
1
```

---

## 💡 Recursive Approach

For every node:

1. If root is NULL → return NULL.
2. If root is one of the target nodes → return root.
3. Search in the left subtree.
4. Search in the right subtree.
5. If both sides return a node → current root is LCA.

---

### C++ Code

```cpp
Node* lowestCommonAncestor(
    Node* root,
    Node* p,
    Node* q)
{
    if(root == NULL || root == p || root == q)
        return root;

    Node* left = lowestCommonAncestor(
        root->left, p, q
    );

    Node* right = lowestCommonAncestor(
        root->right, p, q
    );

    if(left != NULL && right != NULL)
        return root;

    return left != NULL ? left : right;
}
```

### Complexity

```text
Time: O(n)

Space: O(h)
```

